```

This new architecture also means that the evaluation script can use the results given by the tool to decide what messages to send next. For instance, in this `hello` eval, each `output` given by the tool is used as the `input` in the following message. This opens up the possibility of potentially doing more clever evaluations, such as having the evaluation script run some specific numerical optimization algorithm and use the exact gradients returned by the tool to drive the optimization, so we could measure tools not just on their speed at returning gradients but also on their accuracy at returning _good_ gradients.

This PR also adds new GitHub Actions jobs to build Docker images for all the evaluation scripts (currently just one) and also to run each evaluation script with each tool.

